  "slug": "computational-biology-death-5fd7",
  "summary": "Models of ecosystems, cell biology and multi-agent inference now treat emergent boundaries as the object of analysis.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these preprints is that survival-relevant outcomes are governed by emergent, boundary-defining structure rather than by individual components. In random Lotka-Volterra ecosystems, the likelihood of a stable state tracks biomass through emergent self-inhibition and is predicted by biomass and diversity alone. A computational cell-biology argument centres life-death constraints and reports geometric structures separating survival-outcome regions. In simulated multi-agent active inference, flocks form emergent Markov blankets, respond faster to a predator, and hold collective knowledge absent from individual birds. What is new is the explicit framing of emergent boundaries as the object of analysis. The studies differ in domain and methods, so this emphasis is shared across the set rather than universal.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-16"
}
